WASM_BUILTIN(wasmDataSegmentInit)

// BigInt ↔ i64 conversion helpers for export/import wrappers.
// wasmBigIntToI64(retBufI, bigint): extracts i64 from BigInt, writes lo/hi
// to retBufI[0]/[1], returns 0.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmBigIntToI64)
// wasmI64ToBigInt(lo, hi): converts split lo/hi to BigInt.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmI64ToBigInt)

// A table slot is a triple -- the internal closure, its interned type id and
// the Exported Function wrapping both -- spread over three parallel arrays.
// Every write from generated Wasm code goes through one of the two helpers
// below, which is what keeps the three in step. (The JS-API
// WebAssembly.Table.prototype methods still write their arrays directly; they
// are converted in a later change.)
// wasmTableGetSlot(exportedArr, idx): read one slot's Exported Function, or
// null. Not a plain property read: the array comes from a table import, so
// script chooses it, and an accessor at an index would otherwise run user JS
// inside a Wasm function body.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmTableGetSlot)
// wasmTableSetSlot(funcsArr, typesArr, exportedArr, idx, exportedFnOrNull):
// write one slot. The closure and type id are derived from the Exported
// Function's internal properties, so they cannot disagree with it; a value
// that is neither null nor an Exported Function is a TypeError. A refused
// write (a frozen array) is an error, not a silent no-op, and the type id is
// cleared first so a failure part-way leaves the slot uncallable.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmTableSetSlot)
// wasmTableCopySlots(dstFuncs, dstTypes, dstExported, srcFuncs, srcTypes,
// srcExported, dst, src, count): copy a range of slots across all three
// arrays, handling overlap.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmTableCopySlots)

// Bulk table operations (N.2). The three that write table slots --
// wasmTableFill, wasmTableInit and wasmTableGrow -- do it through
// wasmTableSetSlot's implementation, so the slot triple stays consistent;
// wasmElemDrop writes the element-segment array rather than a table.
// wasmTableFill(funcsArr, typesArr, exportedArr, idx, val, count, isFuncRef):
// fill table entries with val.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmTableFill)
// wasmTableInit(funcsArr, typesArr, exportedArr, elemSegs, segIdx, dst, src,
// count, isFuncRef): copy from element segment into a table.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmTableInit)
// wasmElemDrop(elemSegs, segIdx): mark an element segment as dropped.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmElemDrop)
// wasmTableGrow(funcsArr, typesArr, exportedArr, delta, fillVal, maxEntries,
// actualMax): grow table by delta entries, filling with fillVal. Returns old
// size on success, -1 on failure.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmTableGrow)

// Import type validation (Category 4).
// wasmLinkError(message): creates and throws a WebAssembly.LinkError with the
// given message string. Used by Wasm-generated IR during instantiation.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmLinkError)
// wasmInternType(typeString): map a structural Wasm function-type string to a
// process-wide stable integer id, so call_indirect can compare type identity
// across modules with a plain integer compare.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmInternType)
// wasmLinkTable(importVal, declaredIsFuncRef): the brand check that replaced
// the __wasm_funcs__/__wasm_types__/__wasm_exported__ publications. Returns
// the table's three backing arrays and its own maximum as
// [funcs, types, exported, max], or null if `importVal` is not a genuine
// WebAssembly.Table (a dyn_vmcast, which no object literal and no forged
// prototype can satisfy) or if the importing module declared a non-funcref
// element type, which no constructible table can satisfy.
// The arrays are the table's internal fields themselves, NOT copies: two
// modules importing one table must write the same slots.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmLinkTable)
// wasmLinkMemory(importVal): the brand check that replaced the
// __wasm_type__/__wasm_min__/__wasm_max__ publication on a memory. Returns
// [currentPages, max, buffer], or null if `importVal` is not a genuine
// WebAssembly.Memory (a dyn_vmcast, which no object literal and no forged
// prototype can satisfy). currentPages is the buffer's size, so it follows
// every grow; max is -1 when the memory declared none; the buffer is returned
// so that the views the module builds are over the very buffer whose size was
// validated.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmLinkMemory)
// wasmLinkGlobal(importVal, expectedValType, expectedMutable): the brand
// check that replaced the __wasm_type__ publication on a global. Returns the
// matched WebAssembly.Global OBJECT when its type and mutability both match
// the declaration, or undefined for a Global that does not match, or null
// when the argument is not a WebAssembly.Global at all -- three outcomes,
// because they need different diagnostics or different handling by the
// caller. The object is the answer for a snapshot global as much as for a
// live one: the global's VALUE used to be the snapshot answer, which a
// reference-typed global cannot use, `null` and `undefined` being legal
// values of one and the two failure answers both. A caller that wants the
// value fetches it with wasmGlobalGet, which an immutable global import does
// and a mutable one has no use for. expectedValType is a
// JSWebAssemblyGlobal::ValType, or 0xFF for a Wasm type no Global can have.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmLinkGlobal)
// wasmGlobalGet(globalObj) / wasmGlobalSet(globalObj, value): the shared
// state of an imported MUTABLE global, read and written where `.value` used
// to be. A mutable import is not snapshotted -- the module and the host must
// see each other's writes -- so the object is kept and consulted at every
// global.get and global.set. `value` is a CONFIGURABLE accessor on
// WebAssembly.Global.prototype, so doing that through the property let script
// feed the module any value it liked and swallow every write the module made.
// These reach the same internal field the accessor does, past a dyn_vmcast,
// so the sharing survives and the interposition does not.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmGlobalGet)
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmGlobalSet)
// wasmSetFuncInfo(exportedFn, closure, typeId): stamp the two internal
// properties that make `exportedFn` a WebAssembly Exported Function -- the
// internal closure it wraps and the interned id of its signature. Script
// cannot name either property, so the pair is unforgeable BY SCRIPT.
// wasmSetFuncInfo type-checks its arguments anyway, which guards against this
// compiler emitting the wrong thing rather than against an attacker: bytecode
// is trusted, so a CallBuiltin reaching it is one the compiler emitted.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmSetFuncInfo)
// wasmMakeGlobal(valTypeCode, isMutable, valueOrGetter, setterOrUndefined):
// build the WebAssembly.Global a module publishes for an exported global,
// without going through globalThis.WebAssembly.Global -- that is an ordinary
// property read, and replacing it let script decide what a module's exports
// ARE. A defined memory and a defined FUNCREF table already refuse that,
// being brand-checked at construction; the externref table export does not,
// and is a separate defect.
// isMutable selects what the last two arguments mean. When it is true the
// global is LIVE: valueOrGetter and setterOrUndefined are the two closures,
// the global stores no value of its own and reads and writes the module's
// frame slot through them, so an exported mutable global is a two-way view
// rather than a snapshot. When it is false the global is a SNAPSHOT
// initialized to valueOrGetter, and setterOrUndefined is unused.
// The mode used to be read off valueOrGetter being callable, which misread
// an immutable funcref global -- whose snapshot value IS callable -- as a
// live one and refused it.
// valTypeCode is a JSWebAssemblyGlobal::ValType.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmMakeGlobal)
// wasmIsExportedFunction(value) -> boolean: is `value` a WebAssembly
// Exported Function? This is the brand wasmSetFuncInfo stamps, asked as a
// question rather than stamped.
// It exists because generated IR has no way to ask. isWasmExportedFunction is
// a C++ helper, reachable from the JS API and from other builtins but not from
// compiled code, and wasmSetFuncInfo only WRITES the brand; the funcref
// admission tests the compiler emits are generated IR and need the question
// asked. The implementation delegates to the C++ helper, so the two notions of
// the brand cannot drift apart.
// Answers rather than throws for every argument: anything that is not a
// branded object, including a missing argument, is simply false.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmIsExportedFunction)

// The transport for the REFERENCE results of one multi-value call.
//
// A funcref is a JS closure and an externref is an arbitrary JS value, so
// neither fits in the numeric return buffer's ArrayBuffer views. They travel
// in a container allocated by wasmAllocRefBuf, which the caller passes to the
// callee as a hidden argument and then reads back itself.
//
// The container is an ArrayStorage: a GC cell holding traced HermesValue
// elements. It is not a JSObject, so it is not a property container, and the
// generated code reaches it through these builtins rather than through
// indexed property operations. That is what the reference contract asks for --
// the earlier transport was built through globalThis.Array and read and
// written with ordinary indexed property operations, so a replacement
// constructor could discard or substitute a reference on its way through.
//
// wasmAllocRefBuf(slots) -> a container of `slots` elements, all undefined.
// One per multi-value call that has a reference result, sized for the
// CALLEE's signature.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmAllocRefBuf)
// wasmRefBufGet(buf, index) -> the element at `index`.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmRefBufGet)
// wasmRefBufSet(buf, index, value) -> undefined.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmRefBufSet)
// wasmMakeResultArray(v0, v1, ...) -> a fresh JS Array of the arguments.
//
// The public result array of a multi-value export. Built here rather than in
// generated IR because the IR route was globalThis.Array followed by indexed
// property stores: a replaced constructor, or an indexed setter inherited from
// Array.prototype, saw each result on its way into the array and could
// substitute it, drop it, or re-enter an export between two stores. Here the
// array is created and filled through JSArray::setElementAt, which writes the
// array's own indexed storage without consulting a prototype or an accessor.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmMakeResultArray)

// wasmFuncTypeId(value) -> the interned type id of `value`'s signature if it
// is a WebAssembly Exported Function, otherwise undefined.
//
// This is how a FUNCTION IMPORT is type-checked. It used to be checked by
// loading a `__wasm_type__` string the compiler stored on each export
// wrapper, which was wrong three ways at once: the store walked the prototype
// chain, so a Function.prototype setter ran user JS inside instantiation; the
// setter SWALLOWED the store, leaving every wrapper created while it was
// installed unsigned, so an importer handed one fell through to its "any
// callable" path; and what did get stored arrived writable and configurable
// on an object handed to script, so a plain assignment rewrote it afterwards.
//
// The id read here is the one wasmSetFuncInfo already stamps in an internal
// property, which script can neither read, write nor shadow -- there is no
// name to assign to. It is the SAME interning call_indirect compares, so a
// signature is numbered identically in every module.
//
// Answers rather than throws for every argument, like wasmIsExportedFunction:
// anything unbranded, including a missing argument, is undefined. Undefined
// is not a failure -- a plain JS callable legitimately satisfies a function
// import and takes its types from the importer's declaration.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmFuncTypeId)

// wasmMakeTag(code0, code1, ...) -> a WebAssembly.Tag whose parameters are the
// given value-type codes, which are WasmIRGen::globalValTypeCode's and so are
// JSWebAssemblyTag::ValType's.
//
// A module-defined tag used to be a plain object literal carrying its
// signature in an ordinary `__wasm_type__` string property. That store walked
// Object.prototype, so a setter there ran user JS inside instantiation and
// swallowed the store; and what did get stored was writable on an object
// handed to script. Building the tag here instead puts the signature in the
// cell's own C++ field, which has no name to assign to -- and makes a module's
// exported tag a real WebAssembly.Tag, which the spec asks for and which
// `new WebAssembly.Exception(tag, ...)` requires.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmMakeTag)

// wasmCheckTagType(value, code0, code1, ...) -> boolean: is `value` a
// WebAssembly.Tag whose parameters are exactly these value-type codes?
//
// The tag import check. It replaced a string comparison against
// `__wasm_type__`, which had the three failures wasmMakeTag describes, plus a
// fourth of its own: a value carrying no signature at all was accepted as a
// "raw JS value as tag", so swallowing the store on the EXPORTER's side made
// the IMPORTER accept it. A tag import now has to be a genuine tag, which is
// what the spec says and what node does.
WASM_BUILTIN(wasmCheckTagType)

JS_BUILTIN(spawnAsync)
MARK_FIRST_JS_BUILTIN(spawnAsync)
JS_BUILTIN(makeAsyncIterator)
JS_BUILTIN(awaitAsyncGenerator)

#undef NORMAL_OBJECT
#undef NORMAL_METHOD
#undef BUILTIN_OBJECT
#undef BUILTIN_METHOD
#undef PRIVATE_BUILTIN
#undef WASM_BUILTIN
#undef MARK_FIRST_PRIVATE_BUILTIN
#undef JS_BUILTIN
#undef MARK_FIRST_JS_BUILTIN
